.TH COMB 1 "2026" "comb" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
comb \- view, filter and copy log files in a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comb
.RB [ \-e
.IR REGEX ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-color =
.IR MODE ]
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B comb
loads a log file (or piped stdin) into a scrollable pane with severity
highlighting, service-tag coloring, an incremental filter, line marking and
clipboard copy. When stdin is a pipe it is read automatically; a live file is
followed as it grows, surviving truncation and rotation.
.PP
FILE must be a regular file (or stdin). Character devices, FIFOs and sockets
are refused: comb's filter/search model holds every line in memory, so an
unbounded stream (such as
.BR /dev/urandom )
would never finish loading and would buffer without bound. Pipe such a
tool's output in via stdin instead. A single line longer than
.B MAX_LINE_LEN
(config.h; default 4096 bytes) is truncated and marked with a dim
.B …
tail.
.PP
Text is sanitized against terminal control: ANSI escape sequences are
stripped, and any remaining C0/DEL control byte is rewritten
.RB \( cat " -v"
.RB \(^G \(^H " ...) so a log cannot ring the bell, move the cursor or
shift the character set. Valid UTF-8 is left untouched.
.PP
Below
.B MIN_COLS
x
.B MIN_ROWS
(30x6, config.h) the view is unreadable, so comb paints a
.B "too small"
notice in the pane and waits for the window to enlarge; the status bar and
quit/help still work while it does. At 6 rows you get a 4-line pane plus the
status and input bars.
.PP
Filters match literal text by default with smart case (a pattern containing
no uppercase letters matches case-insensitively). A leading
.B ^
or trailing
.B $
anchors to the line start or end.
Ctrl\-R in the filter prompt switches to POSIX ERE mode; the status bar shows
.B (R)
while regex mode is on. Ctrl\-V excludes matching lines instead of showing
them, like
.BR "grep \-v" ;
the status bar shows
.B (!)
while active.
.PP
.B \e
opens a highlight-only search: matching text is colored in place but
the view is not narrowed; n and N jump to the next/previous match,
wrapping at the file edges. While a search is active, the scrollbar on the
right edge marks the track rows whose lines contain hits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " REGEX"
start with
.I REGEX
as the initial (regex-mode) filter
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR
use
.I N
worker threads for parallel file loading and parallel filter/search scans.
The default is online CPUs minus two, clamped to a minimum of 1;
.B \-t 1
runs single-threaded (no worker threads). On a slow disk (SATA HDD) a single
thread saturates the read, so
.B \-t 1
avoids the loader's extra pass; the two-pass parallel load only pays off when
the file is already paged or sits on fast storage.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= MODE
color mode: \fBauto\fR (default), \fBnone\fR, \fBlinux\fR, \fBansi\fR or
\fBtruecolor\fR. \fBnone\fR disables highlighting (also honors
\fBNO_COLOR\fR); \fBlinux\fR uses the fbcon 8/16-color base set
(\fBTERM=linux\fR). Auto reads \fBCOLORTERM\fR and \fBTERM\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
show help and exit
.TP
.B \-
read from stdin; implied when stdin is not a tty
.TP
.B \-\-
treat all following arguments as positional, not options
.SH KEYS
The key bindings below are factory defaults compiled into the binary;
there is currently no runtime remapping configuration. The filter prompt accepts
editing keys; Up/Down/PgUp/PgDn stay live there so
results can be scrolled while typing (terminal wheel scroll arrives as these
keys too).
.TP
.BR j ", " e ", " Enter ", " Down
next line
.TP
.BR k ", " Up
previous line
.TP
.BR d ", " Ctrl\-d ", " PgDn ", " Ctrl\-f
page down
.TP
.BR u ", " Ctrl\-u ", " PgUp ", " b
page up
.TP
.BR g ", " Home
go to top
.TP
.BR G ", " End
go to bottom
.TP
.BR h ", " Left ", " l ", " Right
scroll sideways by 8 columns; 0 or ^ go to the left edge, $ follows the
current line's end
.TP
.B /
open filter prompt (incremental)
.TP
.B \e
open highlight-search prompt; matches are colored in place, the view is
not narrowed. Marks appear on the right-edge scrollbar
.TP
.BR n ", " N
jump to the next / previous search match, wrapping at the file edges;
the view shifts horizontally to reveal matches beyond the screen width
.TP
.B |
clear the highlight search
.TP
.B ?
clear the filter
.TP
.B Esc
leave the prompt (the typed query stays); otherwise clear marks if any,
else clear the filter. While a filter or search is still scanning (the
spinner in the bottom row shows progress), Esc bails out and restores
the previous view
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-z
suspend comb; run
.B fg
to resume in the shell
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-l
redraw the screen
.TP
.BR Space ", " x
mark / unmark the current line and sweep in the direction of the last
vertical move
.TP
.BR c ", " y
copy marked lines (and clear the marks), else the current line. The
OSC 52 escape sequence is always emitted, so copy works over ssh and in
supporting terminals; helper programs (wl\-copy, xclip, pbcopy,
termux\-clipboard\-set) are additionally spawned when available
.TP
.B f
toggle follow mode
.TP
.B w
toggle soft line wrap
.TP
.B r
reload the file from disk
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-h
open the in-pane help page: the full key reference scrolled in the
pane, closes with Ctrl-h, q or Esc
.TP
.B q ", " Ctrl\-c
quit
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
disable syntax coloring when set
.TP
.B COLORTERM
when set to
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
, comb renders with
.B truecolor
unless overridden;
.B TERM
 names (ghostty, kitty, direct, truecolor) are a fallback hint, and
.B TERM=linux
 switches comb to the base 8/16-color palette
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY
gate the clipboard helper search: wl\-copy is only tried when
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set, xclip only when
.B DISPLAY
is. With both set (common under XWayland) the Wayland helper wins because
it is tried first; pbcopy and termux\-clipboard\-set are session\-independent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR less (1)
